> That's suboptimal, since apport times out rather quickly (it's decreased
> every time your pet drops something, and when it's high, they drop stuff
> frequently). So you generally want to increase apport immediately before
> you use it. Also, the amount by which apport is increased is dependent on
> the time elapsed between the pet eating the treat and the time it last
> dropped something. Usually you'll want your pet to drop stuff immediately
> next to you (ie, on the free square). This works better the higher apport
> is, so you'll want to maximise it. Usually it's low enough to be useless
> again after robbing one shop. Read the excellent spoiler on
> http://www.helsinki.fi/~vviitane/spoilers/petsto see the details.

I've tried various pet training strategies and in my experience the
simplest works the best because it works the longest - As long as
you have at least one tripe ration in your open inventory dogs and
cats tend to move towards you.  Once my pet picks up an object
and moves towards me I start moving back into the corridor.  My
pet followed, I displace my pet, I slowly move out until I get the
object, I use displacement to get the pet back in the shop.

For me tossing a tripe ration is what I do with the *second* one.


